WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.237
You know some of your donors need to hear
from you this week.

2
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.553
The hard part is knowing which ones.

3
00:00:05.563 --> 00:00:09.571
Donor Signals scores everyone you have,
every day.

4
00:00:09.581 --> 00:00:11.115
It opens with three numbers.

5
00:00:11.125 --> 00:00:13.745
How many of last year's donors came back.

6
00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:18.000
What you have raised this year, with the
donor count beside it, so a good year on a

7
00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:19.335
shrinking list says so.

8
00:00:19.345 --> 00:00:22.171
And how many first-time donors gave again.

9
00:00:22.181 --> 00:00:23.779
Then six groups.

10
00:00:23.876 --> 00:00:25.695
Every donor sits in one.

11
00:00:26.047 --> 00:00:29.938
And under every count is what is at stake,
so seventeen new gifts worth four thousand

12
00:00:29.948 --> 00:00:33.560
dollars does not look the same as
seventy-one donors slipping away with

13
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.906
seventy-one thousand behind them.

14
00:00:35.916 --> 00:00:38.716
At risk is the one most tools get wrong.

15
00:00:38.726 --> 00:00:41.583
Storyraise measures each donor against
their own rhythm.

16
00:00:41.593 --> 00:00:44.126
A monthly donor who has missed three gifts
is late.

17
00:00:44.136 --> 00:00:47.109
An annual donor who gave in November is
not.

18
00:00:47.119 --> 00:00:49.473
And the list leads with the money.

19
00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:52.327
Every row says why this person, now.

20
00:00:52.337 --> 00:00:55.323
No gift in four months, and they usually
give every two.

21
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.099
Then what you already know:

22
00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:00.443
what they have given, and whether they
read what you send.

23
00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:05.192
Thirty-four of these seventy-one stopped
giving and still open your reports.

24
00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:07.351
Overview reads as a list of people.

25
00:01:07.361 --> 00:01:09.627
Table puts the same donors in a grid you
can sort.

26
00:01:09.637 --> 00:01:12.768
Whichever you use is remembered.

27
00:01:12.802 --> 00:01:14.656
Then the part that is the point.

28
00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:18.887
Two things you can make for the group in
front of you, written for that group.

29
00:01:18.897 --> 00:01:23.195
Record one thank-you for everyone who just
gave, or put together a report showing

30
00:01:23.205 --> 00:01:25.924
what the money is doing.

31
00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:28.304
Open one and you pick how to make it.

32
00:01:28.314 --> 00:01:31.497
A draft written for this group, a
template, or a report you have already

33
00:01:31.507 --> 00:01:32.629
published.

34
00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:37.186
Storyraise saves the group as a list and
opens Send, and each donor gets their own

35
00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:38.638
personalized link.

36
00:01:38.648 --> 00:01:41.187
Further down, the year in full.

37
00:01:41.539 --> 00:01:44.571
Retention by gift size is the table that
changes plans.

38
00:01:44.581 --> 00:01:47.496
Donors under a hundred dollars come back a
third of the time.

39
00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:51.398
Donors over a thousand come back nearly
nine times in ten.

40
00:01:51.408 --> 00:01:54.440
The demo below is the real page with
sample data.

41
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.231
Click through the groups and see what your
own week would look like.
